             <!-- Blog Main Content Here -->
             <!-- Use class 'blog-content' for the wrapper -->
             <!-- CRITICAL: The main image MUST have src="data:image/jpeg;base64," -->
             <img class="blog-hero-image" src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="JEE Main 2026 January 21 Shift 1 &amp; 2 Paper Analysis">
             <div class="blog-content">
                <div class="blog-body">
                    <h1 class="blog-title text-center mb-4">JEE Main 2026 January 21st: Comprehensive Analysis of Shift 1 &amp; 2 Question Papers</h1>
                    <p class="lead text-center text-muted mb-5">Unpacking Day 1 of the JEE Main 2026 Session 1 Examination</p>

                    <p>The Joint Entrance Examination (JEE) Main 2026 Session 1 commenced on January 21, 2026, setting the tone for aspiring engineers across the nation. Thousands of candidates appeared for Paper 1 (B.E./B.Tech) in two shifts, eager to kickstart their journey towards top engineering institutes. With Day 1 successfully concluded, we bring you a detailed analysis of both Shift 1 and Shift 2 question papers, incorporating student feedback and expert opinions.</p>

                    <h2 class="section-title">JEE Main 2026 Day 1: January 21st – A Deep Dive</h2>
                    <p>The first day of JEE Main 2026 saw two distinct shifts, each presenting its own set of challenges and patterns. Understanding these initial trends is crucial for candidates appearing in the subsequent examination days, which are scheduled for January 22, 23, 24, and 28 for Paper 1.</p>

                    <div class="benefit-item">
                        <div class="benefit-title">1. Shift 1 (9 AM – 12 PM) – A Moderate to Tough Start</div>
                        <div class="benefit-content">
                            <p>The JEE Main 2026 Shift 1 exam, held on January 21st morning, was generally rated as moderate to tough by students and subject experts.</p>
                            <div class="subsection-paragraph benefit-card">
                                <p>✅ Approximately 40% of questions were easy, 30% moderate, and 30% involved tricky or lengthy concepts.</p>
                                <p>✅ Many candidates felt the paper was slightly tougher than the January 2025 session, mainly due to the length of Mathematics and certain Chemistry questions.</p>
                            </div>
                            <p><strong>Subject-wise breakdown:</strong></p>
                            <ul>
                                <li><strong>Mathematics:</strong> This section was largely described as difficult and lengthy, making it the most challenging part for many students. High-weightage topics included Calculus, Algebra, Vectors &amp; 3D, and Matrices.</li>
                                <li><strong>Physics:</strong> Physics was generally considered moderate, with some tricky questions. It was reported to be formula-based and manageable, covering topics like electrodynamics, mechanics, heat and thermodynamics, wave optics, and modern physics.</li>
                                <li><strong>Chemistry:</strong> Opinions on Chemistry varied; some experts found it the toughest, while students rated it as easy to moderate and NCERT-driven. There were reports of a lengthy question with seven statements which added to time pressure. Topics included Coordination Compounds, Chemical Bonding, Organic Reaction Mechanism, and Environmental Chemistry.</li>
                            </ul></div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="benefit-item">
                        <div class="benefit-title">2. Shift 2 (3 PM – 6 PM) – Moderate, with Lengthy Maths</div>
                        <div class="benefit-content">
                            <p>The afternoon shift on January 21st was also rated as moderate in difficulty. While some found it slightly more approachable than the morning shift, the Mathematics section continued to be a significant challenge.</p>
                            <div class="subsection-paragraph benefit-card">
                                <p>✅ The paper strictly adhered to the prescribed NCERT and NTA syllabus, though a few students mentioned a question from 'tangent' which might be outside the current syllabus.</p>
                                <p>✅ The average number of attempted questions for well-prepared students ranged from 40 to over 50.</p>
                            </div>
                            <p><strong>Subject-wise breakdown:</strong></p>
                            <ul>
                                <li><strong>Mathematics:</strong> Similar to Shift 1, Mathematics in Shift 2 was lengthy and demanding, maintaining its reputation as the most challenging section.</li>
                                <li><strong>Physics:</strong> This section was reported as moderate, with questions from mechanics, wave optics, electrodynamics, modern physics, and magnetism.</li>
                                <li><strong>Chemistry:</strong> Chemistry was largely considered easy and manageable, primarily NCERT-driven, with a noticeable emphasis on Organic Chemistry.</li>
                            </ul>
                            <p class="highlight-text">Experts suggest that a score of approximately 110 marks may be sufficient to qualify for JEE Advanced eligibility based on preliminary trends from Day 1.</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <h2 class="section-title">Shift Comparison: January 21st, 2026</h2>
                    <p>Here's a quick comparison of the two shifts based on student and expert feedback:</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Parameter</th>
                                <th>Shift 1 (Jan 21, Morning)</th>
                                <th>Shift 2 (Jan 21, Afternoon)</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Overall Difficulty</strong></td>
                                <td>Moderate to Tough</td>
                                <td>Moderate (slightly easier than Shift 1 for many)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Mathematics</strong></td>
                                <td>Difficult &amp; Lengthy</td>
                                <td>Challenging &amp; Lengthy</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Physics</strong></td>
                                <td>Moderate, some tricky/lengthy questions</td>
                                <td>Moderate, concept-based</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Chemistry</strong></td>
                                <td>Varied (toughest by some experts, easy to moderate by students), lengthy questions reported</td>
                                <td>Easy &amp; NCERT-driven, more Organic Chemistry</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Syllabus Adherence</strong></td>
                                <td>Strictly NCERT, some unique question formats</td>
                                <td>Strictly NCERT, minor syllabus conflict reported by some for 'tangent'</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>

                    <h2 class="section-title">What's New? JEE Main 2026 Exam Pattern &amp; Syllabus</h2>
                    <p>For JEE Main 2026, the National Testing Agency (NTA) has maintained the exam pattern introduced in 2025. This means there are no significant changes to the overall structure or marking scheme.</p>
                    <div class="benefit-item">
                        <div class="benefit-title">Key Aspects of JEE Main 2026 Exam Pattern:</div>
                        <div class="benefit-content">
                            <p>The Computer-Based Test (CBT) for Paper 1 (B.E./B.Tech) comprises Multiple Choice Questions (MCQs) and numerical value answer type questions.</p>
                            <div class="subsection-paragraph benefit-card">
                                <p>✅ In Paper 1 (B.E./B.Tech), Section B consists of 5 numerical questions per subject, all of which are mandatory to attempt. There are no optional questions in this section.</p>
                                <p>✅ The JEE Main 2026 syllabus continues to follow the NCERT curriculum for Classes 11 and 12, covering Physics, Chemistry, and Mathematics comprehensively.</p>
                                <p>✅ Each correct answer generally fetches +4 marks, while a negative marking of -1 is applicable for incorrect answers.</p>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <h2 class="section-title">Crucial Takeaways for Upcoming Shifts</h2>
                    <p>For candidates yet to appear for JEE Main 2026, the analysis of Day 1 provides invaluable insights:</p>
                    <div class="benefit-card">
                        <p>✅ <strong>Master NCERT:</strong> A strong grasp of NCERT concepts remains paramount, especially for Chemistry.</p>
                        <p>✅ <strong>Time Management in Mathematics:</strong> Practice lengthy problems to improve speed and accuracy, as Mathematics continues to be a time-consuming section.</p>
                        <p>✅ <strong>Focus on Fundamentals:</strong> Physics questions are largely concept-based and formula-driven, so reinforce your basics.</p>
                        <p>✅ <strong>Practice Numerical Questions:</strong> Given the mandatory nature of numerical questions in Section B, rigorous practice is essential.</p>
                        <p>✅ <strong>Review Past Papers:</strong> Familiarity with previous year's trends can provide an edge, as the paper difficulty often aligns with them.</p>
                    </div>

                    <p>The JEE Main 2026 Session 1 is off to an intense start. Stay calm, focused, and utilize these insights to fine-tune your preparation. Best of luck to all the aspirants!</p>
                </div>
            </div>
       </div>

            
